1
00:00:01,016 --> 00:00:12,842
[SPEAKER_03]: Well, it was obviously what we spoke yesterday was in relation to the, you know, be able to say something and whether it's the suttas, whether it's personal, impersonal, to convey to the others.

2
00:00:13,482 --> 00:00:17,664
[SPEAKER_03]: But that was in the reference of, you know, running the monastery and helping people and so on.

3
00:00:17,785 --> 00:00:24,488
[SPEAKER_03]: But, the same thing actually, it's even more useful for your own practice.

4
00:00:25,088 --> 00:00:26,249
[SPEAKER_03]: So, even if it's not being

5
00:00:27,603 --> 00:00:39,555
[SPEAKER_03]: Made for public, directed to public, being able to convey, to write down, to explain, to describe, what you think you understood, will be the measure of your understanding.

6
00:00:39,915 --> 00:00:41,857
[SPEAKER_03]: So, that's... that's what it really meant.

7
00:00:41,897 --> 00:00:44,620
[SPEAKER_03]: Don't think you understood something, unless you can write it down.

8
00:00:45,200 --> 00:00:51,225
[SPEAKER_03]: And when somebody says, oh, such and such claims the final knowledge, they say, oh, let's go and question him.

9
00:00:51,985 --> 00:01:03,574
[SPEAKER_03]: In other words, let's go and see what he has to say, because they themselves, being arahants with the final knowledge, can tell what he's describing, whether it's accurate, to what extent it's accurate, and so on.

10
00:01:04,154 --> 00:01:11,940
[SPEAKER_03]: And adopting that as a principle, to be able to convey it, to write it down, to explain it to others, something like that,

11
00:01:19,221 --> 00:01:28,138
[SPEAKER_03]: into that kind of state of, oh, well, I understood what I need to understand, or I am practicing towards understanding, I just can't put it in words.

12
00:01:29,160 --> 00:01:31,263
[SPEAKER_03]: I often hear that from people, which kind of results in

13
00:01:33,116 --> 00:01:44,280
[SPEAKER_03]: Spending decades in robes and not being able to formulate an intelligible sentence when it comes to dhamma, without resorting to, like, you know, some mystical views or just parroting, oh, well, this agent says this.

14
00:01:44,480 --> 00:01:47,100
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah, but what is your understanding of that?

15
00:01:47,641 --> 00:01:48,361
[SPEAKER_03]: Can you convey it?

16
00:01:48,421 --> 00:01:49,161
[SPEAKER_03]: Can you explain it?

17
00:01:49,901 --> 00:01:58,404
[SPEAKER_03]: And that also then brings to that second point of being able to... Can you just speak about what you're understanding, how you're understanding, in front of others?

18
00:01:59,150 --> 00:02:00,291
[SPEAKER_03]: Or, is that already an issue?

19
00:02:00,431 --> 00:02:03,692
[SPEAKER_03]: Because, if it's an issue, then it should be recognized as an issue.

20
00:02:03,712 --> 00:02:07,814
[SPEAKER_03]: Like, what's... Why, why do I fear if somebody disagrees with me?

21
00:02:07,914 --> 00:02:10,435
[SPEAKER_03]: Again, I'm not using a new will or something.

22
00:02:10,475 --> 00:02:12,836
[SPEAKER_03]: Obviously, you just don't go around preaching for no reason.

23
00:02:13,817 --> 00:02:14,777
[SPEAKER_03]: But that's not what I'm talking about.

24
00:02:14,797 --> 00:02:23,261
[SPEAKER_03]: I'm talking about that... Oh, I don't want to say something, because, like, you know, if other person disapproves with it, my mind will be bothered.

25
00:02:23,700 --> 00:02:25,762
[SPEAKER_03]: Or I might start regretting what I said.

26
00:02:25,982 --> 00:02:28,443
[SPEAKER_03]: It's like, well, yeah, that's how irrational mind works.

27
00:02:28,744 --> 00:02:29,764
[SPEAKER_03]: That's how doubt works.

28
00:02:30,785 --> 00:02:41,093
[SPEAKER_03]: So, are you not saying and not pushing things in the direction of understanding, interrogation and investigation, because you're afraid you might look like a fool to yourself later on?

29
00:02:42,133 --> 00:02:46,757
[SPEAKER_03]: And, eh... if so, then you realize, no, that's actually not a valid reason to not do it.

30
00:02:47,792 --> 00:02:56,297
[SPEAKER_03]: So, you have to accept that responsibility and that risk that, yeah, your own mind might start turning against you and annoying you and doubting and so on.

31
00:02:56,897 --> 00:03:07,883
[SPEAKER_03]: But the only reason it can do that is because you're trying to get away with it, get away with lack of clarity, get away with taking the stand when it comes to your own views.

32
00:03:08,423 --> 00:03:11,085
[SPEAKER_03]: And if you are taking the stand when it comes to your own views,

33
00:03:11,707 --> 00:03:15,028
[SPEAKER_03]: Then, you will feel vulnerable to the extent that your views are not clear.

34
00:03:15,748 --> 00:03:19,150
[SPEAKER_03]: And that's the thing that will make you practice, that will make you clarify.

35
00:03:34,715 --> 00:03:37,196
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it encourages responsibility.

36
00:03:39,484 --> 00:03:43,345
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah, but it also does come down to the actual doing it.

37
00:03:44,325 --> 00:03:58,608
[SPEAKER_03]: I mean, yeah, there is a truth in, yes, I don't feel qualified to teach, but that does not disqualify you from being able to convey in a non-teaching manner what you understood, how you understood it, and to what extent, and question it and interrogate it.

38
00:03:59,328 --> 00:04:06,950
[SPEAKER_03]: And that's how you will be able to teach properly, because you will clarify things for yourself, and then teaching others will be a side effect of that.

39
00:04:07,475 --> 00:04:08,516
[SPEAKER_02]: That's not why you're doing this.

40
00:04:08,536 --> 00:04:10,518
[SPEAKER_03]: You're doing it because that's how understanding is gained.

41
00:04:11,919 --> 00:04:16,283
[SPEAKER_03]: So, for example, an exercise we can do now.

42
00:04:18,765 --> 00:04:30,496
[SPEAKER_03]: It's like something... yesterday I came across that sutta where the Buddha talks about his views that self... suffering is created by oneself.

43
00:04:31,533 --> 00:04:37,036
[SPEAKER_03]: Or, suffering is created by another, or suffering is created by both, or suffering is created by neither.

44
00:04:37,976 --> 00:04:41,658
[SPEAKER_03]: So, the Buddha said all four views are wrong.

45
00:04:41,898 --> 00:04:43,559
[SPEAKER_03]: It's the contact, i.e.

46
00:04:43,639 --> 00:04:44,119
[SPEAKER_03]: phassa.

47
00:04:44,440 --> 00:04:48,982
[SPEAKER_03]: So, pressure means suffering is there, depending on the reason, but it's not valid.

48
00:04:50,618 --> 00:04:59,386
[SPEAKER_03]: But then, you could rightly sort of ask, so, wouldn't it be more correct to think that its suffering is created by you?

49
00:04:59,947 --> 00:05:04,011
[SPEAKER_03]: You know, through your own negligence, ignorance, craving, and so on.

50
00:05:05,632 --> 00:05:07,194
[SPEAKER_00]: So, how should that be understood then?

51
00:05:08,335 --> 00:05:09,215
[SPEAKER_00]: It's the wrong order.

52
00:05:09,436 --> 00:05:11,377
[SPEAKER_00]: The idea that suffering is created by you.

53
00:05:11,578 --> 00:05:12,418
[SPEAKER_03]: By you, yeah?

54
00:05:13,559 --> 00:05:14,180
[SPEAKER_03]: So, in what sense?

55
00:05:14,320 --> 00:05:16,322
[SPEAKER_03]: So, imagine now I'm a

56
00:05:18,066 --> 00:05:21,368
[SPEAKER_03]: I'm familiar with the suttas, but I would...

57
00:05:21,748 --> 00:05:23,389
[SPEAKER_03]: I read that it doesn't make sense.

58
00:05:24,489 --> 00:05:26,831
[SPEAKER_03]: So, I asked you the question, you say it's the wrong order.

59
00:05:26,851 --> 00:05:29,572
[SPEAKER_03]: So, how shall I understand the right order then?

60
00:05:32,934 --> 00:05:36,977
[SPEAKER_03]: Just in your own words, whatever you would say to some random person that asks you that question.

61
00:05:43,678 --> 00:05:44,198
[SPEAKER_00]: Proceeded.

62
00:05:44,298 --> 00:05:46,080
[SPEAKER_00]: I mean, I won't say this right.

63
00:05:46,120 --> 00:05:49,002
[SPEAKER_00]: This isn't how I would... No, no.

64
00:05:49,022 --> 00:05:50,082
[SPEAKER_00]: That's the whole point.

65
00:05:50,703 --> 00:05:53,324
[SPEAKER_00]: I'm saying this in a very, kind of, abstract way.

66
00:05:53,445 --> 00:05:57,908
[SPEAKER_00]: I would, with somebody who's new, who's like... then I would probably just, like, talk in a very different way.

67
00:05:58,148 --> 00:06:03,451
[SPEAKER_00]: But, yeah, this phenomena of you, in inverted commas, is preceded by

68
00:06:06,173 --> 00:06:11,396
[SPEAKER_00]: Things which I have no control over, therefore, I'm not myself.

69
00:06:11,416 --> 00:06:13,076
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah, in the nutshell, that's exactly what it is.

70
00:06:13,096 --> 00:06:17,919
[SPEAKER_03]: So, even like the craving, the ignorance and stuff, that's not your creation.

71
00:06:18,959 --> 00:06:19,979
[SPEAKER_03]: You're subjected to it.

72
00:06:20,680 --> 00:06:23,481
[SPEAKER_03]: Yes, you're accepting it, welcoming it, acting out of it.

73
00:06:24,461 --> 00:06:28,723
[SPEAKER_03]: That's why you remain bound to the results of craving, to the results of ignorance.

74
00:06:29,164 --> 00:06:32,245
[SPEAKER_03]: But if you were to look at yourself, you don't create that phenomenon.

75
00:06:32,585 --> 00:06:34,286
[SPEAKER_03]: It did not, it does not originate from you.

76
00:06:36,107 --> 00:06:39,530
[SPEAKER_03]: And in the same sense, as the Sutta says, suffering is dependent on your reason.

77
00:06:39,551 --> 00:06:40,571
[SPEAKER_03]: That's exactly what it refers to.

78
00:06:40,632 --> 00:06:55,826
[SPEAKER_03]: So, yeah, practically speaking, like somebody who completely, you know, doesn't have any regard for the Noble Ones, no recognition of the Suttas, no virtue, it's less wrong for them to think you are responsible.

79
00:06:55,926 --> 00:06:56,406
[SPEAKER_03]: It's better.

80
00:06:56,546 --> 00:07:00,250
[SPEAKER_03]: So, all of, you know, the wrong views are not all equally wrong.

81
00:07:00,930 --> 00:07:05,895
[SPEAKER_03]: They're wrong, because, yes, fundamentally, they don't point to the right thing, but some of them are less wrong.

82
00:07:06,496 --> 00:07:08,838
[SPEAKER_03]: And this would be one of the less wrong views.

83
00:07:09,359 --> 00:07:16,967
[SPEAKER_03]: So, yes, it's better to think that it's all created by you, because then you will leave yourself no excuse but to try and deal with it.

84
00:07:17,287 --> 00:07:22,753
[SPEAKER_03]: If you start dealing with it rightly, you'll get to understand then that, oh, okay, so to that extent it's not created by me.

85
00:07:23,572 --> 00:07:28,259
[SPEAKER_03]: And, obviously, the whole sutta, on the basis of the answer the Buddha gave, it becomes obvious.

86
00:07:28,300 --> 00:07:29,782
[SPEAKER_03]: Okay, he's talking in the ultimate sense.

87
00:07:29,822 --> 00:07:30,543
[SPEAKER_03]: So, it's like, no, no, no.

88
00:07:31,104 --> 00:07:36,492
[SPEAKER_03]: Presence of suffering is due to the presence of craving, or presence of contact, presence of pressure.

89
00:07:37,093 --> 00:07:38,415
[SPEAKER_03]: And, yes, that's not something you do.

90
00:07:39,372 --> 00:07:40,533
[SPEAKER_03]: That's something you're subjected to.

91
00:07:41,114 --> 00:07:45,058
[SPEAKER_03]: But being ignorant in regard to who you're subjected to, you assume the ownership, act and so on.

92
00:07:45,318 --> 00:07:50,244
[SPEAKER_03]: And then, as a result of that mistake already, you get to think you are the one who created suffering.

93
00:07:50,584 --> 00:07:54,148
[SPEAKER_03]: So, even your view of suffering is the result of your...

94
00:07:55,769 --> 00:07:59,572
[SPEAKER_03]: Ignorance, in regard to that pressure, that is dependent on the...

95
00:07:59,592 --> 00:08:00,993
[SPEAKER_00]: The nature of that pressure, yeah.

96
00:08:01,133 --> 00:08:01,693
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah, yeah.

97
00:08:01,753 --> 00:08:04,315
[SPEAKER_03]: So, so then, okay, so now that's clear to me.

98
00:08:04,515 --> 00:08:09,319
[SPEAKER_03]: So, how can then I train, train myself in regard to that pressure?

99
00:08:09,639 --> 00:08:20,647
[SPEAKER_03]: But do I just take any pressure, or is there any, like, a set of prerequisites I gotta meet in order for that pressure to be endured rightly, or I just find things that bother me and I do them?

100
00:08:21,087 --> 00:08:21,907
[SPEAKER_03]: How do I go about it?

101
00:08:23,685 --> 00:08:26,146
[SPEAKER_03]: How would I train myself in regard to that pressure so I don't suffer?

102
00:08:27,847 --> 00:08:34,590
[SPEAKER_00]: The statement I gave before was that this phenomenon of self is preceded by things that are outside of my control.

103
00:08:34,870 --> 00:08:42,973
[SPEAKER_00]: So, we could look at examples of those things that could help you develop and establish that understanding.

104
00:08:44,234 --> 00:08:46,715
[SPEAKER_00]: So, for example, how are you feeling right now?

105
00:08:47,435 --> 00:08:47,735
[SPEAKER_03]: Okay.

106
00:08:48,215 --> 00:08:48,775
[SPEAKER_03]: But...

107
00:08:49,496 --> 00:08:52,497
[SPEAKER_03]: So, does that mean I can just, at any point in my life,

108
00:08:53,672 --> 00:08:54,852
[SPEAKER_03]: Don't matter what I'm getting up to.

109
00:08:55,532 --> 00:08:56,833
[SPEAKER_03]: Sit down and ask that question.

110
00:08:57,773 --> 00:08:57,933
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

111
00:08:58,033 --> 00:08:59,113
[SPEAKER_03]: And go in the right direction.

112
00:09:00,574 --> 00:09:04,215
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, it's... it will be abstract if... Why?

113
00:09:04,475 --> 00:09:05,395
[SPEAKER_03]: Why would it be abstract?

114
00:09:05,575 --> 00:09:07,276
[SPEAKER_03]: What do I need to do so it's not abstract?

115
00:09:10,056 --> 00:09:10,877
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah, it would be abstract.

116
00:09:10,897 --> 00:09:11,757
[SPEAKER_03]: That's exactly my point.

117
00:09:12,317 --> 00:09:16,218
[SPEAKER_03]: So, how can I then do it without it remaining abstract?

118
00:09:19,275 --> 00:09:20,055
[SPEAKER_03]: What is the...

119
00:09:20,315 --> 00:09:22,076
[SPEAKER_03]: I mean, others, if they have ideas, they can say it.

120
00:09:22,456 --> 00:09:25,077
[SPEAKER_03]: Well, sense, restraint, virtue... Yeah, yeah.

121
00:09:26,077 --> 00:09:27,117
[SPEAKER_03]: And that's why it's not optional.

122
00:09:28,298 --> 00:09:35,640
[SPEAKER_03]: That's why, like, even if people think the dhamma without sense, restraint and virtue, they're not thinking the dhamma.

123
00:09:35,720 --> 00:09:38,581
[SPEAKER_03]: And that's... that brings to that other point of, like...

124
00:09:39,081 --> 00:09:42,822
[SPEAKER_03]: I think we mentioned that in our recording, when you were sitting with us the other day.

125
00:09:42,882 --> 00:09:46,363
[SPEAKER_03]: It was like, when people think, oh, as long as I make any effort,

126
00:09:47,304 --> 00:09:56,449
[SPEAKER_03]: Towards the dhamma, in terms of thinking, pondering, doesn't matter in what order of things I do it, I'm on the path of the dhamma, because I'm aiming towards liberation.

127
00:09:56,469 --> 00:10:02,432
[SPEAKER_03]: But it's like, well, yeah, abstractly, you are, sure, but in order of doing things matters.

128
00:10:03,013 --> 00:10:16,580
[SPEAKER_03]: And same what we said this morning, like you can have all the lofty ideas, but if you're not enduring things rightly, on the right level, it's going to remain abstraction, that's kind of fueled by the currently suiting and agreeable and supportive circumstances.

129
00:10:18,661 --> 00:10:20,883
[SPEAKER_03]: So, it's important because it's easy to overlook.

130
00:10:21,604 --> 00:10:24,266
[SPEAKER_03]: Okay, yeah, I don't understand that as I go by.

131
00:10:24,346 --> 00:10:24,686
[SPEAKER_03]: No, no, no.

132
00:10:24,706 --> 00:10:40,599
[SPEAKER_03]: You got to do that before and then the pressure and everything else you feel on account of that will be on the right level where you can just then endure it, look at, you know, who is the creator and all those contemplations will have more room, more basis to apply.

133
00:10:42,016 --> 00:10:54,116
[SPEAKER_03]: So, that's why it's... whenever the answer... whenever the Buddha gave the answer to the practice, unless he was, you know, somebody who's already on the path, but he needed some specific clarification, that answer was always on the level of gradual training.

134
00:10:56,119 --> 00:10:56,480
[SPEAKER_03]: He just...

135
00:11:00,339 --> 00:11:02,760
[SPEAKER_03]: Developing that training is the way you have to do it.

136
00:11:03,361 --> 00:11:07,103
[SPEAKER_03]: Yes, you can parallel to your, you know, virtue and senses training.

137
00:11:07,383 --> 00:11:09,004
[SPEAKER_03]: You can keep thinking about it.

138
00:11:09,024 --> 00:11:11,465
[SPEAKER_03]: We say, oh, you must stop thinking until you can do it.

139
00:11:11,505 --> 00:11:12,725
[SPEAKER_03]: But don't put the emphasis on that.

140
00:11:12,745 --> 00:11:13,906
[SPEAKER_03]: Don't think that's the practice.

141
00:11:14,006 --> 00:11:21,250
[SPEAKER_03]: The practice is actually upholding the virtue, restraining the senses and sort of containing your otherwise uncontained mind.

142
00:11:23,714 --> 00:11:28,936
[SPEAKER_01]: So, there's just something that I read this morning and I'll give it to you.

143
00:11:30,077 --> 00:11:31,238
[SPEAKER_01]: Not that I have the answer.

144
00:11:33,078 --> 00:11:53,308
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the Buddha says that one should regard pleasant feeling as pain and painful feeling as a dart and neutral, well, neither pleasant nor painful feeling as anicca.

145
00:11:55,368 --> 00:11:55,628
[SPEAKER_01]: Why?

146
00:11:59,351 --> 00:12:00,452
[SPEAKER_01]: Pleasure has pain.

147
00:12:03,054 --> 00:12:10,960
[SPEAKER_01]: Why must I regard pleasant feeling that I enjoy, why must I regard that as painful?

148
00:12:12,421 --> 00:12:13,262
[SPEAKER_01]: Because it's pleasant.

149
00:12:14,663 --> 00:12:14,943
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

150
00:12:16,104 --> 00:12:18,626
[SPEAKER_01]: What's painful about it?

151
00:12:19,327 --> 00:12:20,567
[SPEAKER_01]: What is painful about it?

152
00:12:20,848 --> 00:12:21,168
[SPEAKER_00]: Pleasure.

153
00:12:24,561 --> 00:12:26,922
[SPEAKER_00]: This is the abstract answer.

154
00:12:26,982 --> 00:12:34,345
[SPEAKER_00]: With an ignorant mind, one will automatically crave more pleasure when one experiences pleasure.

155
00:12:35,946 --> 00:12:41,349
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if there's an ice cream, the thought of the ice cream is pleasant.

156
00:12:42,829 --> 00:12:47,672
[SPEAKER_00]: And the reason why it's pleasant is because it is contained within.

157
00:12:48,680 --> 00:12:57,254
[SPEAKER_00]: This underlying lack of an ice cream, this pain, this uneasiness, that makes that ice cream, that phenomena, pleasurable.

158
00:12:57,855 --> 00:13:02,883
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if one were to, with ignorance, one might think out of the pleasure

159
00:13:04,467 --> 00:13:22,240
[SPEAKER_00]: Is in the ice cream or there there is, yeah, so one is in that sense perceiving feeling, but actually if one were to feel the feeling in that scenario, it would be painful because there would be a sense of wanting more of the pleasure.

160
00:13:22,280 --> 00:13:28,624
[SPEAKER_00]: So it's only it's only pleasurable because one lacks context in relating in regard to that that feeling.

161
00:13:30,386 --> 00:13:31,907
[SPEAKER_01]: Like you have an idea of

162
00:13:33,480 --> 00:13:37,901
[SPEAKER_01]: Like the pleasure, but the the experience of it.

163
00:13:38,562 --> 00:13:45,424
[SPEAKER_01]: You have the idea of like pleasure is nice to have, but the experience of it is... Yeah, pain.

164
00:13:46,604 --> 00:13:51,886
[SPEAKER_02]: But now somebody says, oh, I'm aware of all that, but I'm just having the ice cream nonetheless.

165
00:13:52,106 --> 00:13:54,126
[SPEAKER_02]: I'm just aware of all that.

166
00:13:54,806 --> 00:13:55,827
[SPEAKER_03]: So what's the problem with that?

167
00:13:57,327 --> 00:13:58,488
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, this is the question.

168
00:13:58,868 --> 00:14:00,068
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, I think the problem with

169
00:14:02,930 --> 00:14:09,305
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, the problem is that you are not... that's an abstract theory.

170
00:14:09,706 --> 00:14:12,673
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, I know that pleasure is not to be...

171
00:14:14,563 --> 00:14:16,603
[SPEAKER_01]: Sought-after or pleasure is painful.

172
00:14:17,264 --> 00:14:19,244
[SPEAKER_01]: But you're not acting like that.

173
00:14:19,264 --> 00:14:19,904
[SPEAKER_01]: You are.

174
00:14:19,944 --> 00:14:21,705
[SPEAKER_01]: You are acting as though it is pleasure.

175
00:14:21,725 --> 00:14:24,025
[SPEAKER_01]: So, your idea... You're saying one thing, doing the other.

176
00:14:24,065 --> 00:14:24,965
[SPEAKER_01]: And you're doing this.

177
00:14:24,985 --> 00:14:26,506
[SPEAKER_01]: So, this is what you actually believe.

178
00:14:27,266 --> 00:14:28,786
[SPEAKER_01]: You believe that pleasure is fantastic.

179
00:14:28,806 --> 00:14:31,307
[SPEAKER_02]: So, it is by actions that somebody is known as what they are.

180
00:14:31,327 --> 00:14:31,427
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

181
00:14:31,447 --> 00:14:32,047
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah, exactly.

182
00:14:32,067 --> 00:14:34,928
[SPEAKER_01]: No matter what they're saying, what are they doing, that's their belief.

183
00:14:35,228 --> 00:14:36,368
[SPEAKER_01]: That's the value.

184
00:14:36,508 --> 00:14:37,268
[SPEAKER_03]: So, but...

185
00:14:37,288 --> 00:14:40,249
[SPEAKER_03]: So, like, for example, if somebody who is, again, an outsider,

186
00:14:42,140 --> 00:14:48,041
[SPEAKER_03]: So, maybe they just now start to wander even further and say, okay, but why is the pleasure a problem?

187
00:14:49,801 --> 00:14:55,803
[SPEAKER_03]: Why do I need to free myself from such a harmless pleasure like eating ice cream, for example?

188
00:14:56,903 --> 00:14:57,783
[SPEAKER_03]: It doesn't harm anyone.

189
00:14:59,403 --> 00:15:02,784
[SPEAKER_03]: Why do I need to regard that as pain, so to free my mind from it?

190
00:15:02,884 --> 00:15:03,124
[SPEAKER_03]: Where...

191
00:15:03,224 --> 00:15:04,264
[SPEAKER_03]: I don't see a problem with it.

192
00:15:04,324 --> 00:15:05,044
[SPEAKER_03]: Where's the problem?

193
00:15:05,184 --> 00:15:08,705
[SPEAKER_03]: Where's the issue with allowing such pleasures?

194
00:15:10,491 --> 00:15:15,992
[SPEAKER_00]: The loss of perspective that is... Can you elaborate on that?

195
00:15:18,773 --> 00:15:36,238
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, this understanding that... of the nature of craving leads to this establishment whereby the mind is... isn't moved by, yeah, more particular phenomena, where it's able to become established in

196
00:15:38,368 --> 00:15:43,609
[SPEAKER_03]: So, in other words, it's a weakness you give in to, and the more you give in to, the weaker you become.

197
00:15:44,930 --> 00:15:47,270
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah, by acting out of it, then, exactly.

198
00:15:47,290 --> 00:15:49,631
[SPEAKER_00]: You keep yourself dependable by acting out of it.

199
00:15:49,831 --> 00:15:50,091
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah.

200
00:15:50,451 --> 00:15:53,312
[SPEAKER_01]: So, yeah, that's the whole point.

201
00:15:53,612 --> 00:16:01,014
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the pleasure of having an ice cream, you see, it's not exactly an unwholesome thing in...

202
00:16:02,368 --> 00:16:06,911
[SPEAKER_01]: You know, people don't regard it as something very unwholesome, but it's... is it necessary to do?

203
00:16:07,411 --> 00:16:08,252
[SPEAKER_03]: Well, that's the thing.

204
00:16:08,972 --> 00:16:09,813
[SPEAKER_03]: It's not unwholesome.

205
00:16:09,913 --> 00:16:10,933
[SPEAKER_03]: Ice cream is not unwholesome.

206
00:16:11,514 --> 00:16:13,335
[SPEAKER_03]: Your desire behind is what's unwholesome.

207
00:16:13,975 --> 00:16:21,020
[SPEAKER_03]: So, if you judge your desire by the object of your desire, then you won't necessarily see a problem, because sometimes there is no problem in the object of your desire.

208
00:16:22,489 --> 00:16:26,852
[SPEAKER_03]: But see, unknowingly then, you actually perpetuate the problem, which is the desire.

209
00:16:27,393 --> 00:16:34,338
[SPEAKER_03]: So, if you can eat the ice cream desirelessly, as the Buddha said, the only blameless person, it's not what you eat.

210
00:16:34,378 --> 00:16:39,122
[SPEAKER_03]: It's not if you're vegan, or vegetarian, or whatever else, or eat only the stuff that's fallen from a tree.

211
00:16:39,563 --> 00:16:43,126
[SPEAKER_03]: The only blameless one is the one that eats without a desire, whatever you eat.

212
00:16:44,522 --> 00:16:55,136
[SPEAKER_03]: So, but not seeing that problem, then, through eating something like an ice cream, which in itself it's not an issue, you are carrying the issue, which sooner or later, then, it's going to bite you back.

213
00:16:57,359 --> 00:16:58,201
[SPEAKER_03]: The desire will come.

214
00:16:58,241 --> 00:17:00,984
[SPEAKER_03]: That's why, like we said, suffering is not getting what you want.

215
00:17:02,108 --> 00:17:05,233
[SPEAKER_03]: And the problem of that is not things that don't come to you.

216
00:17:05,273 --> 00:17:12,184
[SPEAKER_03]: The problem is that you want them in the first place, which means, sooner or later, something will not come your way, because you want it.

217
00:17:12,264 --> 00:17:15,770
[SPEAKER_03]: If you didn't want it, weather comes and goes, makes no difference to you.

218
00:17:17,475 --> 00:17:22,298
[SPEAKER_03]: So, that's why even the slightest desire with sensuality and sensual craving is the problem.

219
00:17:23,119 --> 00:17:27,842
[SPEAKER_03]: Because the root of suffering, the root of dependence, is that desire.

220
00:17:28,242 --> 00:17:30,404
[SPEAKER_03]: Not ice cream or this or that.

221
00:17:30,844 --> 00:17:32,085
[SPEAKER_03]: Not pleasure, not pain.

222
00:17:32,505 --> 00:17:33,146
[SPEAKER_03]: In a way, yeah.

223
00:17:33,226 --> 00:17:34,967
[SPEAKER_03]: Well, it's avoiding pain, sure.

224
00:17:36,128 --> 00:17:36,368
[SPEAKER_03]: But...

225
00:17:36,528 --> 00:17:46,735
[SPEAKER_03]: So, the pleasure you get on account of the object of desire you engage with is secondary to the basically pain of the desire you're trying to avoid by doing it.

226
00:17:47,837 --> 00:17:48,738
[SPEAKER_03]: That's where the issue is.

227
00:17:50,459 --> 00:17:52,280
[SPEAKER_02]: But is it possible to eat without desire?

228
00:17:52,681 --> 00:17:54,742
[SPEAKER_03]: Yes, arahant can eat without desire.

229
00:17:54,762 --> 00:17:56,644
[SPEAKER_03]: Yes, but to... with what intention?

230
00:17:56,764 --> 00:17:57,544
[SPEAKER_03]: To sustain the body?

231
00:17:57,845 --> 00:17:58,605
[SPEAKER_03]: To whatever, yeah.

232
00:17:58,625 --> 00:18:01,087
[SPEAKER_03]: He feels hunger in his belly, he puts stuff in it.

233
00:18:01,788 --> 00:18:04,109
[SPEAKER_03]: But isn't that also, in some way, a desire?

234
00:18:04,890 --> 00:18:05,050
[SPEAKER_03]: No.

235
00:18:05,250 --> 00:18:06,071
[SPEAKER_03]: To sustain the body?

236
00:18:06,371 --> 00:18:07,072
[SPEAKER_03]: To live longer?

237
00:18:07,092 --> 00:18:08,193
[SPEAKER_03]: No, that's just an intention.

238
00:18:09,233 --> 00:18:12,196
[SPEAKER_03]: So, arahant can have intentions without a desire.

239
00:18:12,716 --> 00:18:13,597
[SPEAKER_03]: That's the difference there.

240
00:18:13,977 --> 00:18:16,299
[SPEAKER_03]: But yes, if you can see the difference, you'll be at least a sadhapani.

241
00:18:17,262 --> 00:18:20,164
[SPEAKER_03]: So, actually... How can you have intention without a desire?

242
00:18:20,884 --> 00:18:23,965
[SPEAKER_02]: But, so, putujana can intend without desire, but he does not recognize it.

243
00:18:24,005 --> 00:18:25,306
[SPEAKER_02]: He won't recognize it, exactly.

244
00:18:25,366 --> 00:18:26,727
[SPEAKER_02]: But it is possible.

245
00:18:26,747 --> 00:18:26,987
[SPEAKER_02]: No.

246
00:18:27,087 --> 00:18:28,407
[SPEAKER_03]: Well, unknowingly, which means...

247
00:18:28,428 --> 00:18:29,288
[SPEAKER_03]: Yes, exactly.

248
00:18:29,328 --> 00:18:33,190
[SPEAKER_03]: Which means it's not really... like, you need to know it for it to be recognized.

249
00:18:33,210 --> 00:18:35,351
[SPEAKER_00]: Yes, yes, but you don't see... one doesn't see the difference.

250
00:18:35,371 --> 00:18:38,872
[SPEAKER_03]: Well, again, we're not talking about the abstract putujanas, what they might do.

251
00:18:39,153 --> 00:18:43,435
[SPEAKER_03]: We're talking about a personal point of view, which is either putujana or on the way to be an arah

252
00:18:46,516 --> 00:18:53,421
[SPEAKER_03]: Theoretically, yes, Putujana can do that, but that doesn't mean that a Putujana should start thinking now, oh, I am doing that.

253
00:18:53,441 --> 00:18:54,261
[SPEAKER_02]: No, no, no, no.

254
00:18:54,601 --> 00:18:58,364
[SPEAKER_03]: Well, I know you're not implying that, but the way you talk about it implies that.

255
00:18:58,944 --> 00:19:04,288
[SPEAKER_03]: So, if somebody else hears that, aha, so Putujana can then eat without a desire, oh, yeah, I do that sometimes.

256
00:19:04,428 --> 00:19:04,868
[SPEAKER_03]: No, you don't.

257
00:19:05,509 --> 00:19:09,131
[SPEAKER_03]: The only way to do it is to know that you're not doing it.

258
00:19:09,151 --> 00:19:15,135
[SPEAKER_03]: Yes, so the problem is not seeing what Jnanavira says, that intention does not

259
00:19:15,615 --> 00:19:16,897
[SPEAKER_03]: necessarily implied craving.

260
00:19:17,117 --> 00:19:17,497
[SPEAKER_03]: Yes.

261
00:19:17,558 --> 00:19:17,918
[SPEAKER_03]: Exactly.

262
00:19:17,938 --> 00:19:18,178
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah.

263
00:19:18,859 --> 00:19:19,700
[SPEAKER_03]: That needs to be seen.

264
00:19:20,041 --> 00:19:21,442
[SPEAKER_03]: That needs to be thoroughly understood.

265
00:19:22,243 --> 00:19:30,453
[SPEAKER_03]: And that's why it only, the dhamma, and you're talking about dhamma, should only be done on that personal level, because it's already misunderstood.

266
00:19:30,653 --> 00:19:33,577
[SPEAKER_03]: And it's much easier to misunderstand if I make a, again,

267
00:19:34,137 --> 00:19:39,879
[SPEAKER_03]: Theoretically correct statement that, yes, a pūtugena can intend without desire.

268
00:19:40,780 --> 00:19:41,300
[SPEAKER_03]: Accidentally.

269
00:19:41,780 --> 00:19:42,160
[SPEAKER_03]: Exactly.

270
00:19:42,261 --> 00:19:47,263
[SPEAKER_03]: But see, from his point of view, he can't, because all he sees is the desire.

271
00:19:47,323 --> 00:19:52,565
[SPEAKER_03]: So, an arahant can see that sometimes you do it without desire, but that doesn't... his point of view does not apply to you.

272
00:19:53,364 --> 00:20:07,468
[SPEAKER_03]: So, that's why you need to, like, make some special care to not talk in such impersonal senses, because people already are way too keen to assume they're already on the path simply by thinking the dhamma, they're already towards arahantship simply by being inspired, and so on.

273
00:20:09,509 --> 00:20:14,431
[SPEAKER_03]: And, and that's why, yeah, from the personal point of view, that's where the problem is, and that's where the solution is.

274
00:20:14,451 --> 00:20:18,432
[SPEAKER_03]: So, if you start thinking, okay, everything I do is rooted in desire.

275
00:20:19,092 --> 00:20:20,252
[SPEAKER_03]: How do I free myself from it?

276
00:20:21,013 --> 00:20:21,433
[SPEAKER_03]: So, although it's

277
00:20:22,594 --> 00:20:25,036
[SPEAKER_03]: There are some instances you're not rooted in desire.

278
00:20:26,036 --> 00:20:32,941
[SPEAKER_03]: By saying that everything is rooted in desire will kind of force you to be very, very careful and look until you do discern and then you can relax.

279
00:20:33,121 --> 00:20:33,362
[SPEAKER_03]: Sure.

280
00:20:34,122 --> 00:20:38,565
[SPEAKER_03]: But if you try to relax sooner, it's going to end up masking some problem or the other.

281
00:20:39,686 --> 00:20:42,208
[SPEAKER_03]: Oh, well, if you try to relax sooner, you'll be rooted in desire.

282
00:20:44,363 --> 00:20:48,064
[SPEAKER_02]: But have you now answered why these sensual pleasures are painful?

283
00:20:48,524 --> 00:20:50,884
[SPEAKER_02]: Because that was the original question.

284
00:20:50,984 --> 00:20:51,184
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

285
00:20:51,224 --> 00:20:54,125
[SPEAKER_01]: Why is pleasure painful?

286
00:20:54,245 --> 00:20:56,005
[SPEAKER_01]: Or why should it be seen as painful?

287
00:20:56,526 --> 00:20:56,726
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

288
00:20:57,246 --> 00:20:58,526
[SPEAKER_01]: Why should it be seen as painful?

289
00:20:58,766 --> 00:20:59,866
[SPEAKER_02]: Because that's the instruction.

290
00:20:59,886 --> 00:21:01,667
[SPEAKER_02]: It should be regarded as such.

291
00:21:01,727 --> 00:21:05,687
[SPEAKER_02]: So something is pleasurable, but now somebody says you should see it as painful.

292
00:21:06,388 --> 00:21:12,989
[SPEAKER_02]: But the more one understands why that instruction is valid, the better one will be able to regard it as such.

293
00:21:13,888 --> 00:21:16,670
[SPEAKER_02]: Well, you could take it on faith, but it would be a bit of a clash.

294
00:21:16,730 --> 00:21:26,376
[SPEAKER_02]: Like, okay, I experience pleasure, but I should not, and then I feel guilty or something, but... Well, now we're going to talk about Pratyūjana again, but, like, he does not see an escape from suffering.

295
00:21:26,616 --> 00:21:32,580
[SPEAKER_02]: So, the only way he can deal with that which is painful, is to put the sensual pleasures over against it.

296
00:21:32,920 --> 00:21:33,100
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

297
00:21:33,240 --> 00:21:35,642
[SPEAKER_02]: But now, somebody who does see these Four Noble Truths,

298
00:21:36,222 --> 00:21:36,982
[SPEAKER_02]: Right?

299
00:21:37,002 --> 00:21:42,743
[SPEAKER_02]: The origin and where suffering ceases doesn't depend on sensual pleasures in order to allow the suffering to cease.

300
00:21:43,463 --> 00:21:47,844
[SPEAKER_02]: Because he's got this third noble truth, which is cessation of suffering.

301
00:21:48,124 --> 00:21:51,365
[SPEAKER_03]: So, that's exactly the answer there.

302
00:21:51,685 --> 00:21:52,965
[SPEAKER_03]: Why do you need to do that?

303
00:21:53,085 --> 00:21:53,285
[SPEAKER_02]: Yes.

304
00:21:53,625 --> 00:22:00,866
[SPEAKER_02]: And so then, seeing where suffering ceases, this whole domain of sensuality stands out as quite superfluous.

305
00:22:00,886 --> 00:22:03,647
[SPEAKER_02]: It's not necessary anymore to put that over against pain.

306
00:22:04,573 --> 00:22:17,478
[SPEAKER_02]: There might still be the spool, due to how much... how prone somebody was to sensual pleasures, but they are not necessary anymore to deal with this whole burden of existence.

307
00:22:17,658 --> 00:22:23,961
[SPEAKER_03]: So, is that why, then, you should regard prospect of pleasure as painful?

308
00:22:24,061 --> 00:22:25,261
[SPEAKER_02]: Well, this would be my answer, yes.

309
00:22:25,441 --> 00:22:26,181
[SPEAKER_02]: Because it does say so.

310
00:22:26,201 --> 00:22:29,963
[SPEAKER_03]: So, does that mean that this would refer only to somebody with the right view?

311
00:22:31,559 --> 00:22:32,159
[SPEAKER_03]: Which sutra?

312
00:22:32,179 --> 00:22:33,100
[SPEAKER_03]: The one that...

313
00:22:33,120 --> 00:22:33,700
[SPEAKER_03]: It's Sotāpanna.

314
00:22:34,201 --> 00:22:34,601
[SPEAKER_02]: Well, yeah.

315
00:22:34,641 --> 00:22:36,602
[SPEAKER_02]: Well, that sutra makes precisely the distinction.

316
00:22:36,702 --> 00:22:44,246
[SPEAKER_02]: It says an ordinary person has no other way of dealing with arisen suffering other than putting sensual pleasures over against it.

317
00:22:44,666 --> 00:22:47,628
[SPEAKER_02]: But now, here, somebody who sees the dhamma has another way.

318
00:22:48,529 --> 00:22:56,373
[SPEAKER_02]: And so, this is why it should be seen as painful, because the sensual... Well, my understanding is that the sensual pleasures sit on top of the pain for an ordinary person.

319
00:22:56,913 --> 00:22:57,894
[SPEAKER_02]: And so, he first should

320
00:22:58,688 --> 00:23:02,751
[SPEAKER_02]: Learn to not go in that direction, which will make the pain more salient.

321
00:23:02,791 --> 00:23:08,594
[SPEAKER_02]: But that's precisely how you can... well, that's my approach, how one can then discern the origin of that pain.

322
00:23:09,295 --> 00:23:14,798
[SPEAKER_02]: If that's not going to be the prominent thing which sticks out, it's going to be difficult to see how it originates.

323
00:23:16,519 --> 00:23:25,545
[SPEAKER_03]: So, can then an outsider then just start applying the attitude of every pain will be... every pleasure will be regarded as painful?

324
00:23:26,818 --> 00:23:44,969
[SPEAKER_02]: Well, yes, if if he sees every sensual pleasure as painful, whenever he is... whenever there is the desire for sensual pleasure, if he remembers that that is going against the dhamma and will prevent him from seeing the actual origin of suffering... No, he doesn't...

325
00:23:44,989 --> 00:23:52,394
[SPEAKER_03]: I mean, an outsider, someone who doesn't see the origin of suffering, would he benefit from simply what he said first?

326
00:23:53,534 --> 00:23:54,035
[SPEAKER_03]: Attitude?

327
00:23:55,073 --> 00:24:04,306
[SPEAKER_03]: Well, I would say, as as a reason, independence upon the pain.

328
00:24:04,546 --> 00:24:08,611
[SPEAKER_02]: So, there is something that sits sort of below it and that is that is where you want to go.

329
00:24:08,992 --> 00:24:10,995
[SPEAKER_02]: So, don't go in the direction of the sensual pleasure.

330
00:24:11,726 --> 00:24:15,408
[SPEAKER_02]: But go into the direction of the pain that is that is below it.

331
00:24:16,548 --> 00:24:24,892
[SPEAKER_02]: And if you see that that sensual pleasure is your attitude of dealing with that pain, seeing that it depends on it, then that which depends on pain is part of the pain.

332
00:24:25,432 --> 00:24:26,933
[SPEAKER_02]: And so we come to regard it as painful.

333
00:24:27,954 --> 00:24:28,234
[SPEAKER_03]: Right.

334
00:24:29,054 --> 00:24:32,156
[SPEAKER_03]: But yeah, that would be the result if you understand things.

335
00:24:32,416 --> 00:24:32,616
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah.

336
00:24:32,636 --> 00:24:34,457
[SPEAKER_03]: But I'm saying if somebody who doesn't understand,

337
00:24:35,385 --> 00:24:40,688
[SPEAKER_03]: Well, would... could they understand if they just regard pleasure as painful?

338
00:24:40,728 --> 00:24:42,609
[SPEAKER_03]: Would they arrive at the understanding you just described?

339
00:24:42,629 --> 00:24:44,570
[SPEAKER_02]: Well, if somebody has faith, they might.

340
00:24:44,890 --> 00:24:47,932
[SPEAKER_02]: But if they're inclined towards understanding, they might not work on their understanding.

341
00:24:48,152 --> 00:24:48,372
[SPEAKER_02]: Sir?

342
00:24:48,873 --> 00:24:56,497
[SPEAKER_02]: Well, if somebody has faith in the Buddhist instruction, they might just take that as a valid teaching and start regarding things like that.

343
00:24:56,517 --> 00:24:58,558
[SPEAKER_02]: And that might have results.

344
00:24:59,299 --> 00:25:03,641
[SPEAKER_02]: But it's also possible that somebody wants to work on their understanding first, but arrives

345
00:25:07,992 --> 00:25:18,319
[SPEAKER_03]: So, what would happen to somebody who is not familiar with the Buddha's teaching, but for some reason adopts that attitude?

346
00:25:18,599 --> 00:25:23,863
[SPEAKER_03]: Every pleasure is painful, and because of that attitude, he doesn't engage with that pleasure.

347
00:25:24,643 --> 00:25:25,824
[SPEAKER_03]: How far can he take that?

348
00:25:27,625 --> 00:25:31,608
[SPEAKER_02]: Well, he might be able to properly restrain himself in sensual pleasures.

349
00:25:32,921 --> 00:25:33,421
[SPEAKER_02]: What does it mean?

350
00:25:33,821 --> 00:25:34,582
[SPEAKER_03]: Properly restrained.

351
00:25:34,622 --> 00:25:36,022
[SPEAKER_02]: Like, free himself from sensuality?

352
00:25:38,503 --> 00:25:39,703
[SPEAKER_02]: It's just an aversion.

353
00:25:39,903 --> 00:25:40,824
[SPEAKER_02]: Well, yeah.

354
00:25:41,904 --> 00:25:49,287
[SPEAKER_02]: If some ordinary person goes to a Buddha, he has just this teaching and has faith, he might be able to abandon...

355
00:25:49,407 --> 00:25:50,487
[SPEAKER_03]: Even without a Buddha.

356
00:25:50,567 --> 00:25:52,648
[SPEAKER_03]: If an ordinary outsider... Yeah.

357
00:25:53,347 --> 00:26:04,972
[SPEAKER_03]: If the Buddha would adopt this attitude, that every pleasure is actually painful, and sustain that attitude for the duration of the pleasure, the pull of the pleasure that he endures, he would be able to free himself from the assumption of centrality.

358
00:26:05,792 --> 00:26:15,857
[SPEAKER_03]: And that's why the Buddha did say, like, outside of this dispensation, there are samanas and brāhmaṇas that have uprooted essential desires, completely, rightly, not wrongly.

359
00:26:17,205 --> 00:26:26,349
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if you are... your attitude, you're developing this attitude of pleasure, you know, central pleasure, but pleasure itself is painful.

360
00:26:27,590 --> 00:26:33,572
[SPEAKER_02]: Well, central pleasure, because there's also unworldly pleasure, which is... Yeah, but... Yeah, which is the wholesome pleasure.

361
00:26:33,632 --> 00:26:34,232
[SPEAKER_03]: But, yeah...

362
00:26:34,272 --> 00:26:38,494
[SPEAKER_03]: Which can only be discerned after you have thoroughly withdrawn yourself from the central pleasure.

363
00:26:38,634 --> 00:26:39,295
[SPEAKER_03]: Exactly.

364
00:26:39,315 --> 00:26:44,277
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's... it's not something that you can... You said unworldly pleasure, the pleasure of renunciation.

