556
00:39:45,077 --> 00:39:47,438
[SPEAKER_03]: That needs to be done to arrive at the answer.

557
00:39:47,898 --> 00:39:55,922
[SPEAKER_03]: If you think you can access the answer without having met this training of the right order, it won't be the answer you're getting.

558
00:39:56,862 --> 00:39:58,723
[SPEAKER_03]: And people don't realize how important that is.

559
00:39:59,463 --> 00:40:01,704
[SPEAKER_03]: So, how do I go about contemplating anatta?

560
00:40:02,185 --> 00:40:07,428
[SPEAKER_03]: Well, by keeping the precepts, dwelling the sense restraint, watchful guarding of the sense doors.

561
00:40:08,028 --> 00:40:19,375
[SPEAKER_03]: Then, the suffering you start experiencing within that type of container, that has potential to be seen as anicca, felt as dukkha rightly, and then experience the whole experience as anatta.

562
00:40:20,176 --> 00:40:22,197
[SPEAKER_03]: If you don't do that setup,

563
00:40:23,037 --> 00:40:24,900
[SPEAKER_03]: Whatever you feel won't be what you think you're feeling.

564
00:40:25,321 --> 00:40:29,208
[SPEAKER_03]: And that's like, as I would have said in, maybe, Moolapare or somewhere... No, another sutra.

565
00:40:45,041 --> 00:40:53,622
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah, and it does say there, that even if he is experiencing painful waking feelings due to striving, he's not going to get the result.

566
00:40:57,361 --> 00:41:14,249
[SPEAKER_02]: If somebody does some retreat somewhere, and has aching knees, and aching back, and aching head and shoulder, and stuff like this, on the counter, sitting for 10 hours a day, but they're not observing any precepts, then, then, one might assume that there's a sense of discipline involved in that, but, but, as you would say, this is on the wrong level.

567
00:41:14,269 --> 00:41:14,809
[SPEAKER_03]: So, there you go.

568
00:41:14,829 --> 00:41:20,192
[SPEAKER_03]: But, now, actually, to play devil's advocate, but they are observing the precepts during their retreat.

569
00:41:20,532 --> 00:41:20,752
[SPEAKER_03]: Yes.

570
00:41:20,892 --> 00:41:21,693
[SPEAKER_03]: But, where's the problem?

571
00:41:22,273 --> 00:41:23,353
[SPEAKER_03]: So, what you said is correct.

572
00:41:23,854 --> 00:41:24,234
[SPEAKER_03]: Exactly.

573
00:41:24,614 --> 00:41:26,055
[SPEAKER_03]: All the pain they're going through

574
00:41:27,152 --> 00:41:28,833
[SPEAKER_03]: It's not going to result in the knowledge.

575
00:41:29,173 --> 00:41:29,353
[SPEAKER_02]: No.

576
00:41:30,034 --> 00:41:33,916
[SPEAKER_03]: Although, at the same time, when they're going through the pain, they are keeping the precepts.

577
00:41:34,557 --> 00:41:35,157
[SPEAKER_03]: Well, yes.

578
00:41:35,678 --> 00:41:39,660
[SPEAKER_03]: But why wouldn't result then in the knowledge?

579
00:41:40,060 --> 00:41:43,643
[SPEAKER_02]: Well, because... well, actually...

580
00:41:44,726 --> 00:41:58,036
[SPEAKER_02]: Well, okay, so you are saying if they do observe the precepts during that retreat... Well, if somebody has been observing the precepts beforehand and has been restrained beforehand, then it's not impossible that in fact they get results on that retreat.

581
00:41:58,776 --> 00:42:00,718
[SPEAKER_02]: But what I was trying to say is that ordinarily...

582
00:42:02,408 --> 00:42:04,889
[SPEAKER_02]: People do not do that in their, let's say, household life.

583
00:42:04,949 --> 00:42:10,031
[SPEAKER_02]: And then, when they come to retreat, most of the time, there's not much emphasis on the virtue either.

584
00:42:10,051 --> 00:42:20,555
[SPEAKER_02]: So, the idea is then that, okay, we cannot be bothered because this is all like, you know, hīnayāna, this is all discipline, narrow, small, minor, and so on.

585
00:42:20,915 --> 00:42:28,870
[SPEAKER_02]: So, the discipline then is not in... on the level of sīla, but the discipline is only conforming to a schedule which tells you when to sit and to walk and all the rest of it.

586
00:42:28,891 --> 00:42:31,816
[SPEAKER_03]: So, basically, the precepts are conditional.

587
00:42:32,910 --> 00:42:34,111
[SPEAKER_03]: Due to the circumstances.

588
00:42:34,491 --> 00:42:37,133
[SPEAKER_03]: Circumstances are making them keep the precepts.

589
00:42:37,154 --> 00:42:37,394
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

590
00:42:37,734 --> 00:42:38,555
[SPEAKER_03]: That's exactly why.

591
00:42:38,975 --> 00:42:41,337
[SPEAKER_02]: Well, if they keep the precepts... No, no.

592
00:42:41,457 --> 00:42:50,584
[SPEAKER_03]: Even if they keep the perfect precepts, if it's rooted in circumstances, not their decision to put the precepts and responsibility for the intention behind the action first.

593
00:42:51,025 --> 00:42:54,968
[SPEAKER_03]: That's why sharp, wracking, painful feeling will not result in the knowledge.

594
00:42:55,528 --> 00:43:00,893
[SPEAKER_03]: Even if they kept the precepts before, even if they keep the precepts after their retreat, even if they keep during the retreat.

595
00:43:01,333 --> 00:43:04,094
[SPEAKER_03]: Their view puts the precept second.

596
00:43:04,114 --> 00:43:10,136
[SPEAKER_03]: Their view assumes the precept as a conditional duty, so I get this direct access to what I'm trying to get.

597
00:43:11,037 --> 00:43:15,918
[SPEAKER_03]: And that's why always, that's why always those retreats that are always based on that are wrong, because of that.

598
00:43:16,519 --> 00:43:23,581
[SPEAKER_03]: Because people arrive at it thinking they are directly going for something, while what they should put first is actually conditionally second.

599
00:43:24,001 --> 00:43:24,401
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

600
00:43:24,681 --> 00:43:26,102
[SPEAKER_03]: And same goes for the monks, by the way.

601
00:43:26,642 --> 00:43:28,523
[SPEAKER_03]: Somebody can be in rows for 50 years,

602
00:43:30,110 --> 00:43:30,871
[SPEAKER_03]: Pure virtue.

603
00:43:31,231 --> 00:43:32,192
[SPEAKER_03]: And still be a kutujana.

604
00:43:33,153 --> 00:43:35,616
[SPEAKER_03]: And that's exactly what Śrīla Bhāgavatam Paramahā says.

605
00:43:35,656 --> 00:43:41,462
[SPEAKER_03]: Doing it conditionally on the level of a sense of duty, and not putting it first as your practice.

606
00:43:41,782 --> 00:43:44,725
[SPEAKER_03]: Because if you do, then you get the weight of your intention.

607
00:43:45,826 --> 00:43:52,473
[SPEAKER_03]: Because now, it doesn't matter if the rule doesn't specify I can or cannot do this.

608
00:43:52,553 --> 00:43:52,894
[SPEAKER_03]: I know

609
00:43:53,381 --> 00:43:56,824
[SPEAKER_03]: That it's rooted in the intention affected with desire.

610
00:43:56,984 --> 00:43:57,885
[SPEAKER_03]: Thus, I won't do it.

611
00:43:58,865 --> 00:44:05,531
[SPEAKER_03]: But see, if I assume the precepts, two hundred and... five hundred and seventy of them, doesn't matter.

612
00:44:05,651 --> 00:44:10,435
[SPEAKER_03]: But I assume them as the external definitions, then anything not defined, I can do.

613
00:44:10,635 --> 00:44:11,656
[SPEAKER_03]: And I don't have to think about.

614
00:44:12,717 --> 00:44:13,998
[SPEAKER_03]: And there is no practice for you at all.

615
00:44:15,179 --> 00:44:20,264
[SPEAKER_03]: And that's, unfortunately, what's considered purity today in, like, monasteries and temples.

616
00:44:20,344 --> 00:44:26,050
[SPEAKER_03]: It's the idea of being overly defined about every little aspect and sticking to it.

617
00:44:27,051 --> 00:44:30,474
[SPEAKER_03]: Which automatically results in, okay, putting your intention second.

618
00:44:31,315 --> 00:44:35,980
[SPEAKER_03]: Because the letter is important, the authority is important, the tradition is important, the external definition is important.

619
00:44:36,831 --> 00:44:46,075
[SPEAKER_03]: So, even if you spend your lifetime doing it, and enduring lots of pain on account of it, there will be no wisdom arising on account of sharp and painful feeling that you're going through.

620
00:44:46,856 --> 00:44:50,838
[SPEAKER_03]: Because you're still keeping the perversion in your core, what comes first.

621
00:44:50,858 --> 00:44:51,078
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

622
00:44:51,258 --> 00:44:56,981
[SPEAKER_02]: If sharp breaking feelings arise at all, somebody could also just fall asleep within that context.

623
00:44:57,181 --> 00:44:58,341
[SPEAKER_03]: Sure, sure, sure.

624
00:44:58,381 --> 00:45:01,643
[SPEAKER_02]: In a sense, pleasure ball is the one kind of pleasure.

625
00:45:01,723 --> 00:45:01,963
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah.

626
00:45:07,445 --> 00:45:22,168
[SPEAKER_03]: But then, if you realize that that's what comes first, putting the virtue, so to reveal your intentions for which you are already assuming responsibility, then the idea of going on a retreat or not, well, it's... it's circumstantial.

627
00:45:22,348 --> 00:45:26,249
[SPEAKER_03]: Like, I might go and attend a retreat just to change the environment.

628
00:45:26,409 --> 00:45:28,530
[SPEAKER_03]: Either way, internally, my practice remains the same.

629
00:45:28,650 --> 00:45:29,250
[SPEAKER_03]: Exactly, yes.

630
00:45:30,688 --> 00:45:32,029
[SPEAKER_01]: Not giving in to greed.

631
00:45:32,109 --> 00:45:32,490
[SPEAKER_03]: Exactly.

632
00:45:32,830 --> 00:45:33,871
[SPEAKER_03]: There's no difference.

633
00:45:34,431 --> 00:45:36,273
[SPEAKER_03]: There is no difference whether I'm here or there.

634
00:45:36,573 --> 00:45:38,134
[SPEAKER_03]: My work is the same.

635
00:45:38,174 --> 00:45:39,015
[SPEAKER_03]: Virtue comes first.

636
00:45:39,435 --> 00:45:42,558
[SPEAKER_03]: Intentions revealed on account of my sense restraint is my work.

637
00:45:43,799 --> 00:45:49,283
[SPEAKER_03]: So, yes, if I can have a time to, you know, spend in some quiet environment, I'll do that.

638
00:45:50,003 --> 00:45:55,588
[SPEAKER_03]: Because it's obviously going to be like less harassing me than if I stay in the middle of the city.

639
00:45:56,527 --> 00:45:57,747
[SPEAKER_03]: But either way, I gotta do...

640
00:45:57,807 --> 00:46:00,148
[SPEAKER_03]: I can't do the practice only when I'm... That's not a practice.

641
00:46:00,168 --> 00:46:06,450
[SPEAKER_03]: That's just reacting, basically, as a sort of, like, out of aversion towards the busy city.

642
00:46:07,150 --> 00:46:10,251
[SPEAKER_03]: So, I'm just trying to get rid of discomfort by doing a retreat or whatever else.

643
00:46:12,611 --> 00:46:12,852
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

644
00:46:14,672 --> 00:46:18,913
[SPEAKER_03]: And now we're just, you know, we're still... we're talking just on the level of the pure environment of retreat.

645
00:46:19,013 --> 00:46:23,194
[SPEAKER_03]: We're not getting even into the... what views are being taught on the retreats and stuff.

646
00:46:23,274 --> 00:46:24,115
[SPEAKER_02]: That's an issue in itself.

647
00:46:24,215 --> 00:46:25,475
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah, that's coming in another talk.

648
00:46:28,310 --> 00:46:44,323
[SPEAKER_02]: Now, when you say that it's important to put the intentions first, always, in the context of the sīla, and to feel the weight and the burden of that, can you connect that with the right practice of mindfulness of breathing, which is also an exercise of putting intention first?

649
00:46:45,764 --> 00:46:46,925
[SPEAKER_02]: Or is that a bit of a leap?

650
00:46:47,246 --> 00:46:47,526
[SPEAKER_02]: How?

651
00:46:47,686 --> 00:46:48,487
[SPEAKER_02]: How would you go about it?

652
00:46:49,350 --> 00:46:50,071
[SPEAKER_02]: How would you connect it?

653
00:46:50,511 --> 00:46:51,172
[SPEAKER_02]: Well, tell me more.

654
00:46:51,432 --> 00:47:02,525
[SPEAKER_02]: If I sit down, and I'm breathing in, and I'm breathing out, and I know that I'm breathing in and breathing out, then that seems to me to be... that's a bodily act, a bodily intention.

655
00:47:03,987 --> 00:47:05,929
[SPEAKER_02]: Breathing in, breathing out, it's something that I'm doing.

656
00:47:06,530 --> 00:47:08,012
[SPEAKER_03]: Oh, right, so you're referring that just...

657
00:47:09,309 --> 00:47:13,151
[SPEAKER_03]: General sense of what would be the right application of ānāpānasati.

658
00:47:13,331 --> 00:47:13,651
[SPEAKER_03]: Exactly.

659
00:47:13,731 --> 00:47:14,491
[SPEAKER_02]: Oh, yeah, yeah, definitely.

660
00:47:14,651 --> 00:47:22,034
[SPEAKER_02]: And I was trying to connect that with the idea, because this seems to be one of the more popular discourses, because there's this tendency to teach methods and techniques and so on.

661
00:47:22,114 --> 00:47:22,294
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

662
00:47:22,354 --> 00:47:27,356
[SPEAKER_02]: We don't need to go into that, but once we have established... Then it's not a method, it's not a technique.

663
00:47:27,396 --> 00:47:27,937
[SPEAKER_02]: Yes.

664
00:47:27,977 --> 00:47:34,119
[SPEAKER_02]: And we see that the right virtue, as we have just said, is about putting the intentions first.

665
00:47:37,420 --> 00:47:53,484
[SPEAKER_02]: Trying to find out if somebody has understood all of that, and they sit in that retreat context, and then somebody widely expounds ānāpānasati, would that not be seeing on a deeper level that there are intentions going on, and that the whole practice is to see...

666
00:47:53,524 --> 00:47:55,705
[SPEAKER_02]: But he won't... it probably wouldn't happen.

667
00:47:56,263 --> 00:47:58,767
[SPEAKER_03]: In a retreat setting, it will happen in solitude.

668
00:47:59,929 --> 00:48:02,372
[SPEAKER_02]: You don't go sit in a room full of people to watch your breath.

669
00:48:02,693 --> 00:48:03,694
[SPEAKER_03]: That's already a factor.

670
00:48:03,955 --> 00:48:04,495
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah, of course.

671
00:48:04,515 --> 00:48:11,686
[SPEAKER_03]: So, if somebody would have seen and developed virtue rightly and already has the right view, all he would need to do is have an empty room.

672
00:48:12,635 --> 00:48:13,415
[SPEAKER_03]: Yes.

673
00:48:13,435 --> 00:48:14,195
[SPEAKER_03]: That's the extent.

674
00:48:14,276 --> 00:48:14,816
[SPEAKER_03]: Close the door.

675
00:48:14,916 --> 00:48:17,597
[SPEAKER_03]: That's the extent of solitude needed.

676
00:48:17,797 --> 00:48:19,217
[SPEAKER_03]: And then, yes, exactly.

677
00:48:19,237 --> 00:48:23,578
[SPEAKER_03]: Then the practice would be, knowingly, he breathes in, knowingly, I breathe out, knowingly, I breathe in.

678
00:48:24,058 --> 00:48:25,399
[SPEAKER_03]: Yes.

679
00:48:25,479 --> 00:48:27,479
[SPEAKER_03]: And it's very much on the level of the intentions.

680
00:48:28,800 --> 00:48:33,001
[SPEAKER_03]: And then, knowingly, I'll intend to breathe like this, knowingly, I'll breathe out like that.

681
00:48:33,621 --> 00:48:39,103
[SPEAKER_03]: Knowingly, I'll basically, while I'm breathing in, I shall intend to relinquish.

682
00:48:40,205 --> 00:48:43,087
[SPEAKER_03]: Things, ideas, activities that my mind holds.

683
00:48:44,369 --> 00:48:45,469
[SPEAKER_03]: And that's the practice, yeah.

684
00:48:45,529 --> 00:48:47,751
[SPEAKER_03]: It's a practice of discerning the neutral intention.

685
00:48:48,852 --> 00:48:59,982
[SPEAKER_03]: But to practice discerning the neutral intention, while you don't even see wholesome and wholesome intention, while your virtue is defined by circumstances and externally assumed rule, it does not apply to you.

686
00:49:00,342 --> 00:49:00,522
[SPEAKER_03]: Yes.

687
00:49:00,702 --> 00:49:05,967
[SPEAKER_03]: That's why ānāpānasati was taught to those who were at least sotāpannas and still had work to do.

688
00:49:13,874 --> 00:49:16,741
[SPEAKER_03]: And there was another one, I mentioned that in the talk we did in Sri Lanka.

689
00:49:17,964 --> 00:49:19,668
[SPEAKER_03]: Are you ready to tell?

690
00:49:19,708 --> 00:49:20,931
[SPEAKER_03]: Was that Arita?

691
00:49:20,951 --> 00:49:21,753
[SPEAKER_03]: Venerable Arita?

692
00:49:23,840 --> 00:49:33,243
[SPEAKER_03]: When the Buddha says ānāpānasati will bring much benefit to those who do it, to the monks who do it, and Venerable Arita says, yes, Lord, I do ānāpānasati.

693
00:49:33,864 --> 00:49:34,824
[SPEAKER_03]: And he says, how do you do it?

694
00:49:35,404 --> 00:49:46,548
[SPEAKER_01]: He said, knowingly I breathe in, knowingly I breathe out, and because of it, I have... Before he says that, I've abandoned essential desires for past essential pleasures.

695
00:49:46,728 --> 00:49:50,249
[SPEAKER_01]: I've abandoned essential desires for future essential pleasures.

696
00:49:50,269 --> 00:49:50,409
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

697
00:49:54,202 --> 00:49:55,523
[SPEAKER_03]: Well, I'm free from them here and now.

698
00:49:55,683 --> 00:50:07,355
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, he abandons those desires for pleasures of the past and future and he, what's the word, calms down in regards to any resistance that he's felt now.

699
00:50:07,415 --> 00:50:08,075
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, yeah, the patiga.

700
00:50:08,095 --> 00:50:09,056
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, yeah, yeah.

701
00:50:09,096 --> 00:50:09,917
[SPEAKER_01]: Internal resistance.

702
00:50:10,137 --> 00:50:10,958
[SPEAKER_01]: In regards to the body.

703
00:50:11,098 --> 00:50:12,900
[SPEAKER_01]: He's abandoned those essential pleasures.

704
00:50:13,781 --> 00:50:14,441
[SPEAKER_01]: What do you feel?

705
00:50:14,642 --> 00:50:17,264
[SPEAKER_01]: What you're left with is the pain of existence.

706
00:50:21,385 --> 00:50:23,546
[SPEAKER_01]: Mindfully I breathe in, mindfully I breathe out.

707
00:50:23,646 --> 00:50:28,248
[SPEAKER_02]: And then the Buddha says, that is indeed it, but he then goes a bit further.

708
00:50:28,268 --> 00:50:32,209
[SPEAKER_03]: Because there is obviously the subtler aspects of conceit and appropriation left there.

709
00:50:34,708 --> 00:50:42,216
[SPEAKER_03]: You realize, so, first of all, eh... ānāpānasati wouldn't have been applying to him if he wasn't at least a sotāpanna.

710
00:50:42,336 --> 00:50:48,142
[SPEAKER_03]: Second of all, he was already... he freed himself from sensuality by seeing the danger and so on.

711
00:50:48,403 --> 00:50:51,105
[SPEAKER_03]: And, obviously, knowingly breathing in, knowingly breathing out.

712
00:50:51,686 --> 00:50:56,411
[SPEAKER_03]: He calmed down the patiga of the resistance to your own existence, which then uprooted that

713
00:50:57,796 --> 00:51:03,380
[SPEAKER_03]: So, in itself, that's all you need to do.

714
00:51:03,600 --> 00:51:05,121
[SPEAKER_03]: Knowingly breathe in, knowingly breathe out.

715
00:51:05,281 --> 00:51:06,262
[SPEAKER_03]: That's your ānāpānasati.

716
00:51:06,962 --> 00:51:14,527
[SPEAKER_03]: And when, then, you become free from sensuality, established in the right view, then the rest of the ānāpānasati can apply to you.

717
00:51:18,948 --> 00:51:27,551
[SPEAKER_02]: So, if somebody comes along, here, or somewhere else, and says, one day, can you instruct me in ānāpānasati, then... You say, yes.

718
00:51:28,272 --> 00:51:29,152
[SPEAKER_03]: And then you start...

719
00:51:29,172 --> 00:51:33,794
[SPEAKER_03]: Teach them the... tell them that they have to spend years of developing virtue, and that will be their ānāpānasati.

720
00:51:36,280 --> 00:51:46,944
[SPEAKER_03]: And in this puzzle, you say, well, the ānāpānasati, you know, famously expounded in the Ānāpānasati Sutta, was taught by the Buddha to those who were at least Sotāpanna or higher.

721
00:51:47,364 --> 00:51:50,305
[SPEAKER_03]: So, until you reach that, this does not apply to you.

722
00:51:50,886 --> 00:51:53,507
[SPEAKER_02]: But can I sit down knowingly breathing, knowingly without?

723
00:51:53,607 --> 00:51:53,827
[SPEAKER_03]: No.

724
00:51:54,447 --> 00:51:55,447
[SPEAKER_03]: They cannot, you would say.

725
00:51:56,008 --> 00:51:56,968
[SPEAKER_03]: No, definitely they cannot.

726
00:51:57,068 --> 00:51:58,149
[SPEAKER_02]: Not with the right context.

727
00:51:58,329 --> 00:51:59,889
[SPEAKER_03]: No, there won't be the ānāpānasati.

728
00:51:59,909 --> 00:52:01,610
[SPEAKER_03]: That would be meditation with centrality.

729
00:52:02,080 --> 00:52:02,460
[SPEAKER_03]: Of course.

730
00:52:02,480 --> 00:52:05,442
[SPEAKER_03]: That will be premeditating, undermeditating, overmeditating, mismeditating.

731
00:52:05,662 --> 00:52:08,703
[SPEAKER_03]: There will be waiting, waiting, waiting for that to happen.

732
00:52:09,944 --> 00:52:10,104
[SPEAKER_03]: No?

733
00:52:10,244 --> 00:52:10,344
[SPEAKER_03]: No.

734
00:52:10,364 --> 00:52:11,425
[SPEAKER_03]: Because you don't see intentions.

735
00:52:11,445 --> 00:52:17,307
[SPEAKER_03]: How can you do ānāpānasati rightly when you haven't been sufficiently cultivating the recognition of intentions behind your actions?

736
00:52:17,968 --> 00:52:20,089
[SPEAKER_03]: Where wholesome and unwholesome is rooted?

737
00:52:21,970 --> 00:52:28,513
[SPEAKER_03]: So, any ānāpānasati, by the farthest stretch of imagination, doesn't matter.

738
00:52:28,693 --> 00:52:28,953
[SPEAKER_03]: But still,

739
00:52:31,322 --> 00:52:38,384
[SPEAKER_03]: Centrality, trying to get the pleasure, trying to avoid the pain, trying to avoid responsibility, trying to avoid dealing with things that need to be dealt with beforehand.

740
00:52:39,125 --> 00:52:42,186
[SPEAKER_03]: It would be, give me this, so I don't have to actually do the work.

741
00:52:43,106 --> 00:52:45,207
[SPEAKER_03]: Give me the practice, so I don't have to practice.

742
00:52:46,027 --> 00:52:48,448
[SPEAKER_01]: So, yeah, why do you want to practice ānāpānasati?

743
00:52:50,208 --> 00:52:50,828
[SPEAKER_01]: What's the reason?

744
00:52:50,848 --> 00:52:53,669
[SPEAKER_01]: Because you... do you want some... something from it?

745
00:52:54,029 --> 00:52:55,430
[SPEAKER_01]: Do you want some future?

746
00:52:56,280 --> 00:52:56,580
[SPEAKER_01]: Pleasure?

747
00:52:56,941 --> 00:52:58,502
[SPEAKER_01]: Do you desire some future pleasure?

748
00:52:58,822 --> 00:53:00,103
[SPEAKER_02]: I want to...

749
00:53:00,183 --> 00:53:02,465
[SPEAKER_03]: Okay, let's... let's... let's advocate again.

750
00:53:02,705 --> 00:53:03,766
[SPEAKER_03]: So, well, no.

751
00:53:04,366 --> 00:53:08,410
[SPEAKER_03]: I want to practice ānāpānasati, because it calms me down.

752
00:53:09,510 --> 00:53:10,451
[SPEAKER_03]: And calmness is good.

753
00:53:10,731 --> 00:53:11,732
[SPEAKER_03]: So, what's the problem then?

754
00:53:11,952 --> 00:53:14,694
[SPEAKER_03]: What's the problem with me doing ānāpānasati, if it calms me down?

755
00:53:18,558 --> 00:53:19,378
[SPEAKER_03]: Anyone can say.

756
00:53:22,061 --> 00:53:32,346
[SPEAKER_00]: Didn't say before, the teaching of the ānāpānasati, that you abandoned future... Well, yeah, I'll do that later.

757
00:53:32,787 --> 00:53:35,108
[SPEAKER_03]: But, factually, when I sit...

758
00:53:36,008 --> 00:53:45,813
[SPEAKER_03]: I'm not a sotāpanna, I don't know the Buddhist teaching that much, but I went on a retreat and I was taught ānāpānasati, and when I sit and do it, it calms me down, factually.

759
00:53:46,154 --> 00:53:47,074
[SPEAKER_03]: What is wrong with that?

760
00:53:47,814 --> 00:53:48,715
[SPEAKER_03]: Why is that a problem?

761
00:53:50,662 --> 00:53:52,463
[SPEAKER_03]: So, that means I can do ānāpānasati.

762
00:53:53,043 --> 00:53:57,924
[SPEAKER_03]: No, unless you... as long as you don't regard that to be what the Buddha is talking about.

763
00:53:58,364 --> 00:53:59,664
[SPEAKER_03]: So, then it's not ānāpānasati.

764
00:53:59,764 --> 00:54:02,385
[SPEAKER_03]: But it is the Buddha's ānāpānasati that I've been taught and I'm doing.

765
00:54:03,005 --> 00:54:03,605
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah.

766
00:54:03,625 --> 00:54:04,765
[SPEAKER_03]: So, there is a danger.

767
00:54:04,785 --> 00:54:06,226
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's either not the ānāpānasati.

768
00:54:06,686 --> 00:54:06,866
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

769
00:54:06,886 --> 00:54:10,787
[SPEAKER_02]: So, somebody might wrongly assume that they are practicing rightly and are making progress.

770
00:54:10,847 --> 00:54:11,587
[SPEAKER_02]: That's the danger.

771
00:54:11,867 --> 00:54:12,087
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah.

772
00:54:12,867 --> 00:54:15,048
[SPEAKER_03]: What else is the more acute danger?

773
00:54:17,676 --> 00:54:19,458
[SPEAKER_03]: Let's put ānāpānasati aside.

774
00:54:20,218 --> 00:54:24,562
[SPEAKER_03]: What is the danger of me valuing my calmness?

775
00:54:24,782 --> 00:54:27,404
[SPEAKER_03]: Because it's sensuality.

776
00:54:27,524 --> 00:54:31,107
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not seeing the pleasure as painful.

777
00:54:31,507 --> 00:54:31,748
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

778
00:54:32,128 --> 00:54:32,448
[SPEAKER_00]: What else?

779
00:54:32,468 --> 00:54:32,768
[SPEAKER_00]: Sorry?

780
00:54:33,939 --> 00:54:38,266
[SPEAKER_03]: It's denying, in a sense, disturbance, or trying to deal with it.

781
00:54:38,866 --> 00:54:39,828
[SPEAKER_03]: Dealing with it, exactly.

782
00:54:40,028 --> 00:54:40,990
[SPEAKER_03]: It's management.

783
00:54:41,090 --> 00:54:42,171
[SPEAKER_03]: It's a condition of calmness.

784
00:54:42,191 --> 00:54:42,372
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah.

785
00:54:43,053 --> 00:54:45,496
[SPEAKER_03]: So, you are calm when you get...

786
00:54:45,516 --> 00:54:49,643
[SPEAKER_03]: So, it's like, okay, so you value calmness, which means you're not uprooting the disturbance.

787
00:54:51,308 --> 00:54:58,050
[SPEAKER_03]: Like, yes, you know, it's nice to be calm, but the real question you should ask, but why am I disturbed in the first place?

788
00:54:58,911 --> 00:54:59,671
[SPEAKER_03]: That's the problem.

789
00:55:00,091 --> 00:55:11,435
[SPEAKER_03]: But if I just do ānāpānasati for the calmness that I took at face value as something that is always good, means I'm taking simultaneously for the face value that I will keep getting disturbed.

790
00:55:13,207 --> 00:55:16,189
[SPEAKER_03]: So, am I liable to future disturbance once I calm myself?

791
00:55:16,289 --> 00:55:16,569
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah.

792
00:55:16,969 --> 00:55:18,330
[SPEAKER_03]: Well, that's the problem.

793
00:55:18,450 --> 00:55:20,091
[SPEAKER_03]: Not that you were disturbed or calm.

794
00:55:20,151 --> 00:55:21,953
[SPEAKER_03]: The problem is that you can get disturbed.

795
00:55:23,093 --> 00:55:39,444
[SPEAKER_03]: And it always ties in with the view that, see, by valuing this calmness per se, means I don't kind of... well, means all the views that I have, on account of what is the disturbing aspect for me, will remain unchanged.

796
00:55:40,322 --> 00:55:44,464
[SPEAKER_03]: Because as long as I have access to the technique of calmness, that's how I deal with it.

797
00:55:45,225 --> 00:55:51,928
[SPEAKER_03]: It's like, but... Did the disturbance come from you not watching your nostrils sufficiently enough?

798
00:55:52,068 --> 00:55:53,589
[SPEAKER_03]: Is that how disturbance enters you?

799
00:55:55,090 --> 00:55:57,591
[SPEAKER_03]: So, when you calm down, how does that disturbance come back?

800
00:55:57,731 --> 00:55:59,092
[SPEAKER_03]: Why do you lose that calmness?

801
00:56:00,993 --> 00:56:04,114
[SPEAKER_03]: And then we're back on to, ah, intentions behind the actions.

802
00:56:04,755 --> 00:56:07,116
[SPEAKER_03]: You cannot become disturbed accidentally.

803
00:56:09,050 --> 00:56:10,491
[SPEAKER_03]: It cannot just happen to you.

804
00:56:10,511 --> 00:56:12,333
[SPEAKER_03]: You always open the door for disturbance.

805
00:56:12,453 --> 00:56:12,893
[SPEAKER_03]: How?

806
00:56:13,854 --> 00:56:15,635
[SPEAKER_03]: Acting out of greed, aversion, delusion.

807
00:56:15,755 --> 00:56:16,156
[SPEAKER_03]: That's it.

808
00:56:16,416 --> 00:56:19,378
[SPEAKER_03]: No other gateway for disturbance to enter you.

809
00:56:20,379 --> 00:56:28,726
[SPEAKER_03]: So, all your efforts that are focused and revolve around calming yourself, are the efforts of just plastering over a wound that remains unchanged.

810
00:56:29,647 --> 00:56:33,210
[SPEAKER_03]: And it always ties in with the views that disturbance comes from the world.

811
00:56:33,856 --> 00:56:36,697
[SPEAKER_03]: Well, it's the busy life, it's the busy... none of that...

812
00:56:36,737 --> 00:56:41,257
[SPEAKER_03]: I mean, the robbers chopping off your limbs should not be disturbing you if you did the dhamma rightly.

813
00:56:43,818 --> 00:56:50,799
[SPEAKER_03]: So, through your practice of calmness, taken at face value, you are maintaining your wrong views of where the disturbance actually is.

814
00:56:51,959 --> 00:56:59,961
[SPEAKER_03]: So, you only... the only way to force yourself to deal with the disturbance rightly, then, is to not give in to the temptation to manage it.

815
00:57:01,018 --> 00:57:01,879
[SPEAKER_03]: Through calmness.

816
00:57:02,299 --> 00:57:06,542
[SPEAKER_03]: So, it's never good to do it, unless you have the right view.

817
00:57:06,942 --> 00:57:20,991
[SPEAKER_03]: You'll be closer to calming yourself rightly by pondering on the qualities of the Buddha, the dhamma, the saṅgha, the sensory strength, the virtue, taking responsibility for your action and being watchful and careful before you do things by body, speech and mind.

818
00:57:21,071 --> 00:57:23,733
[SPEAKER_03]: That will result in the factual samādhi, i.e.

819
00:57:24,113 --> 00:57:28,756
[SPEAKER_03]: being less likely, less liable to be disturbed, even amidst the disturbances.

820
00:57:29,857 --> 00:57:32,858
[SPEAKER_03]: And, funny enough, that's what the Buddha says in the suttas.

821
00:57:32,918 --> 00:57:39,101
[SPEAKER_03]: If you're not a sotāpanna, what your samādhi is, it's like recollecting the Buddha, the dhamma, the saṅgha, the virtue.

822
00:57:40,422 --> 00:57:47,485
[SPEAKER_03]: Will unify your mind and basically make it unmovable in the face of things that want to move it.

823
00:57:48,474 --> 00:57:50,636
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah, and generosity and the deities.

824
00:57:51,097 --> 00:57:51,497
[SPEAKER_03]: Exactly.

825
00:57:51,537 --> 00:57:52,818
[SPEAKER_03]: Sometimes it says also.

826
00:57:53,099 --> 00:58:05,270
[SPEAKER_03]: So, develop those contexts actively, obviously with the virtue to match it, and that will be the factual samādhi, that will be the factual peace and calmness that's not rooted in just trying to manage the pain.

827
00:58:06,472 --> 00:58:12,778
[SPEAKER_03]: And also, it will not be fueling your wrong view further of where the pain and disturbance comes from in the first place.

828
00:58:13,783 --> 00:58:16,747
[SPEAKER_02]: And this is why people keep coming back from one retreat to the next, to the next.

829
00:58:16,787 --> 00:58:16,987
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

830
00:58:17,007 --> 00:58:21,153
[SPEAKER_02]: Because on account of this wrong approach, they become dependent on that calmness.

831
00:58:21,253 --> 00:58:21,493
[SPEAKER_02]: Exactly.

832
00:58:21,513 --> 00:58:24,657
[SPEAKER_02]: So, it's a good marketing tool for just signing up for the next one.

833
00:58:24,717 --> 00:58:28,242
[SPEAKER_03]: It's just that the people that market it are victims themselves, with the same view.

834
00:58:28,362 --> 00:58:29,583
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah, they're not doing it deliberately.

835
00:58:29,603 --> 00:58:30,945
[SPEAKER_03]: They're not doing it deliberately, yeah.

836
00:58:31,506 --> 00:58:31,766
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah.

837
00:58:32,087 --> 00:58:32,347
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah.

